  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:22:29.677Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nYes, the employee is qualified for substituted filing. Under Section 51-A of the NIRC, individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, with correct tax withheld, are not required to file an annual income tax return.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Section 51-A**: \"Substituted Filing of Income Tax Returns by Employees Receiving Purely Compensation Income.\"\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51-A of the NIRC explicitly states that individual taxpayers who earn purely compensation income from a single employer for the entire year, and whose income tax has been correctly withheld by that employer, are exempt from filing an annual income tax return. The employer's certificate of withholding serves as the substitute for the tax return.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis provision simplifies the tax compliance process for employees with straightforward compensation income, reducing the administrative burden of filing a return when the tax obligations have already been met through withholding. It allows employees to rely on their employer's remittance of taxes, ensuring that they do not face additional filing requirements unless their situation changes (e.g., multiple employers or other sources of income).",
